  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nScattered thunderstorms have developed this afternoon across our far\neast and southeast in a region of better instability. Flow is fairly\nlight through the mid levels so storms will continue to be very slow\nmoving and capable of producing locally heavy downpours and isolated\nflooding. The storms are diurnally driven so expect them to \ndissipate as we head into this evening. \n\nA shallow mid/upper level trough will remain in place across the \nGreat Lakes region through this weekend and into early next week, \nwith fairly typical summertime temperatures continuing. Several, \nmainly weaker impulses are forecast to move through the trough \nduring this timeframe, but models are struggling in their timing, \nplacement and overall strength of these. As a result, expect \noccasional chances for showers and storms at times through much of \nthe period -- but it is tough to pinpoint much in the way of \nspecifics. \n\nIn the nearer term, the better chances for precipitation currently \nappear to be in the Friday to Friday night time period as a weak \nsurface boundary approaches from the west. Mid level winds will \npickup somewhat over the next few days, leading to some marginal \ndeeper layer shear. This could lead to the potential for a few \nstronger to possibly severe storms as we head through the end of the \nweek and into the weekend but confidence remains fairly low.",
